# Proof: The Hahn–Banach functional *L* is continuous on ℓ^∞

## Setup

The standard construction alluded to is the following. Let $c \subset \ell^\infty$ be the closed subspace of convergent sequences, equipped with the sup norm $\|x\|_\infty = \sup_n |x_n|$. On $c$ define the linear functional
$$f(x) = \lim_{n\to\infty} x_n.$$
This is a **bounded** (continuous) linear functional on $c$, since
$$|f(x)| = \left|\lim_{n\to\infty} x_n\right| = \lim_{n\to\infty}|x_n| \le \sup_n |x_n| = \|x\|_\infty,$$
so $\|f\|_{c^*} \le 1$. Equality holds because $f(\mathbf{1}) = 1$ and $\|\mathbf{1}\|_\infty = 1$, hence $\|f\|_{c^*} = 1$.

The Hahn–Banach theorem (analytic form for normed spaces) extends $f$ to a linear functional $L$ on all of $\ell^\infty$ satisfying $\|L\|_{(\ell^\infty)^*} = \|f\|_{c^*} = 1$.

## The Hahn–Banach extension is norm-preserving

Recall the precise statement. Let $X$ be a normed space, $M\subseteq X$ a subspace, and $g:M\to\mathbb{R}$ a bounded linear functional. Set the sublinear dominating functional $p(x) = \|g\|_{M^*}\cdot\|x\|$. Then $g(x)\le p(x)$ for all $x\in M$. By Hahn–Banach, there exists a linear extension $G:X\to\mathbb{R}$ with
$$G(x) \le p(x) = \|g\|_{M^*}\|x\| \quad\text{for all } x\in X.$$
Applying this to $-x$ gives $-G(x)=G(-x)\le \|g\|_{M^*}\|x\|$, so
$$|G(x)| \le \|g\|_{M^*}\|x\| \quad\text{for all } x\in X.$$
Therefore $G$ is bounded on $X$ with $\|G\|_{X^*}\le \|g\|_{M^*}$. Since $G$ extends $g$, we have $\|G\|_{X^*}\ge \|g\|_{M^*}$, and consequently
$$\|G\|_{X^*} = \|g\|_{M^*}.$$

## Application to ℓ^∞

Taking $X = \ell^\infty$, $M = c$, $g = f$, we obtain $L:\ell^\infty\to\mathbb{R}$ linear with
$$\|L\|_{(\ell^\infty)^*} = \|f\|_{c^*} = 1 < \infty.$$

A linear functional on a normed space is **continuous if and only if it is bounded** (i.e., has finite operator norm). Since $\|L\|_{(\ell^\infty)^*} = 1 < \infty$, $L$ is bounded, hence continuous.

Concretely, for every $x\in\ell^\infty$,
$$|L(x)| \le \|L\|\cdot\|x\|_\infty = \|x\|_\infty,$$
which is the Lipschitz estimate certifying continuity (in fact $1$-Lipschitz).

## Conclusion

**Yes.** The functional $L$ obtained from the Hahn–Banach theorem is continuous on $\ell^\infty$. The Hahn–Banach extension is norm-preserving: it extends a bounded functional on a subspace to a bounded functional on the whole space with the same finite norm. Boundedness of a linear functional on a normed space is equivalent to continuity, so $L$ is continuous. In the canonical case of extending the limit functional from $c$ to $\ell^\infty$, the extension satisfies $\|L\|=1$, hence $|L(x)|\le\|x\|_\infty$ for all $x\in\ell^\infty$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
